		{
			dependencies: ["1667D9ECE9C63D16"]
			description: ["The &6Photonic Integration Hall&r joins chips by light. The first pass puts a Quantum Photonic Chip on a crossbar and makes the &6Photonic Compute Module&r, the part every later pass builds on. In the photonic integration hall (a circuit 12 program): a Quantum Photonic Chip and a Crossbar on Interposer. The crossbar is a phase-change memory array (four PCM photonic dies and a GST target, in the photonic foundry with ultrapure water and argon) set on an etched glass interposer in an assembler. The photonic tensor core, the next pass of the same hall. Chips and crossbars by ME; the hall makes one module per run, so give it its own pattern provider."]
			hide_dependency_lines: true
			icon: "grindless:photonic_compute_module"
			id: "72559994607D2562"
			rewards: [{ id: "558D4246727E73D3", type: "xp", xp: 150 }]
			shape: "hexagon"
			size: 1.0d
			tasks: [
				{ id: "18029EEF22F1FEAB", item: "grindless:quantum_photonic_chip", type: "item" }
				{ id: "799E6A83C3D4A13A", item: "grindless:crossbar_on_interposer", type: "item" }
				{ id: "548EBDBE468ADBEB", item: "grindless:photonic_compute_module", type: "item" }
			]
			title: "Photonic Compute Module"
			x: 22.0d
			y: 13.0d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["5F3FD5DD9FD7DBAA"]
			description: ["A ring of silicon nitride turns one laser into hundreds of evenly spaced colours, and a lithium niobate layer on it lets a voltage steer them. The &6EO Microcomb&r is the clock of the tensor core. In the TFLN Fab (a circuit 4 program): an annealed damascene ring and an LNOI wafer. The ring is the damascene silicon nitride wafer annealed in the photonic foundry (circuit 10) with ultrapure water and argon. The comb-fed tensor core. Rings and wafers by ME; one comb goes into each tensor core, so keep a small stock."]
			hide_dependency_lines: true
			icon: "grindless:eo_microcomb"
			id: "317DB9E16E50D2F8"
			rewards: [{ id: "5D438DE9F76780E2", type: "xp", xp: 150 }]
			shape: "hexagon"
			size: 1.0d
			tasks: [
				{ id: "1AECEE2F50A951E8", item: "grindless:annealed_damascene_ring", type: "item" }
				{ id: "73A9D4F31E0E47AB", item: "grindless:eo_microcomb", type: "item" }
			]
			title: "Electro-Optic Microcomb"
			x: 22.0d
			y: 15.5d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["3F6512C5FF0EBF55", "72559994607D2562", "317DB9E16E50D2F8"]
			description: ["The third pass of the hall sets the comb on the tensor core, so every multiplication is timed by the same colours. The &6Comb-Fed Tensor Core&r is the arithmetic of the mainframe. In the photonic integration hall (a circuit 13 program): a Photonic Tensor Core and an EO Microcomb. The optical mainframe. Feed the hall by ME from the two stocks; one core per mainframe."]
			hide_dependency_lines: true
			icon: "grindless:comb_fed_tensor_core"
			id: "153F2450D023186E"
			rewards: [{ id: "1C0DE9E8D76A50E6", type: "xp", xp: 180 }]
			shape: "hexagon"
			size: 1.0d
			tasks: [{ id: "31E45C147D8E4A13", item: "grindless:comb_fed_tensor_core", type: "item" }]
			title: "Comb-Fed Tensor Core"
			x: 25.5d
			y: 14.25d
		}
		{
			dependencies: ["153F2450D023186E"]
			description: [
				"The mainframe is the fourth pass of the hall, and four lines feed it."
				"The &6Crossbar on Interposer&r goes in at the first pass. The &6BEUV Driver ASIC&r, with its scanner, mirrors and comb, goes in at the second. The &6EO Microcomb&r, from the TFLN Fab, joins at the third."
				"At the last pass come the &6Six-Hour Memory&r, the &6P:Si Processor&r and two &6Optical Assemblies&r, each made from three optical processors and three quantum memory chips in the photonic foundry (circuit 5). Three more feeders (an EO transceiver, a telecom memory and a pumped EDFA) are the optical processors' own parts."
				"The UIV Lines chart draws the whole of it, and every feeder can be automated apart from the others."
			]
			icon: "gtceu:photonic_integration_hall"
			id: "7349229CF2C0D2C9"
			rewards: [{ id: "53371F1F18F4EF89", type: "xp", xp: 25 }]
			shape: "circle"
			size: 0.75d
			tasks: [{ id: "733763B2509E3CB7", type: "checkmark" }]
			title: "The Feeders of the Mainframe"
			x: 29.0d
			y: 16.75d
		}
		{
